  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n+1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$",
